v² = 2eV/m and v = √(2eV/m). Answer (2) confirmed.\",\n      \"(1): the coefficient 4 is wrong. (3): 1/2 mv² = 2eV gives v = √(4eV/m), so that row is internally consistent but (ア) is wrong. Both explanations are correct.\",\n      \"Dimensions for (4)/(5): eE is a force [N]; energy needs eEd = eV. eE/m is an acceleration [m/s²], and √(m/s²) is not m/s. The explanations are correct.\",\n      \"The table is fully captured as text in the choices, so no figure is needed and figureUrls=[] is appropriate. The page has no electrode diagram, which matches the candidate's figureDescription. reviewedFromPage=[16] matches the page code T1-R 016.\",\n      \"officialReferenceUrls=[]. The explanation relies only on the basic work–energy relation and makes no claim that needs an outside source.\"\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3107,"cache_read_input_tokens":161098,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3107},"output_tokens":734,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d3361800-da1b-4214-b922-18720ab80b0f","uuid":"f4baea84-c282-4d28-987f-9de350a8bf6d","timestamp":"2026-09-24T05:21:47.023Z","request_id":"req_011CfMhjgRbqs5aDE4Whusox"}
